---

<Page
  title={PAGE_TITLE}
  description={PAGE_DESCRIPTION}
  eyebrow="Free tool"
  heading="HTTP Headers."
  intro="Every request and response header that earns its place in production — what it does, a value you would really see on the wire, and the trap that comes with it. Type in the box to filter the whole reference."
  structuredData={[breadcrumbLd, appLd, faqLd]}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-6">
    <StickyNote tone="yellow" tilt={-1.5} class="max-w-md">
      <p class="mono mb-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
        ▸ Static page
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-snug font-bold">
        The whole reference is server-rendered HTML. The only script here is the
        filter box, and it never sends anything anywhere.
      </p>
    </StickyNote>
  </section>

  <div id="http-headers-root">
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-10">
      <div id="http-headers-controls" hidden>
        <NeoCard tone="paper" shadow="md" class="space-y-4">
          <div>
            <label
              for="http-headers-input"
              class="mono mb-2 block text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
            >
              Filter headers and directives
            </label>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
              <input
                id="http-headers-input"
                type="search"
                placeholder="cors, cache-control, hsts, client ip…"
                spellcheck="false"
                autocomplete="off"
                class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-sm min-w-0 flex-1 rounded-[4px] border-[3px] px-4 py-3 font-mono text-base font-bold focus:outline-none"
              />
              <button
                type="button"
                id="http-headers-clear"
                hidden
                class="border-ink bg-bg-alt shadow-neo-xs hover:bg-yellow hover:shadow-neo-sm inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-[4px] border-[2.5px] px-3 py-2.5 text-xs font-bold tracking-wide uppercase transition-all hover:-translate-x-0.5 hover:-translate-y-0.5"
              >
                <Icon name="lucide:x" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" aria-hidden="true" />
                Clear
              </button>
            </div>
            <p
              id="http-headers-count"
              aria-live="polite"
              class="mono mt-2 text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70"
            >
              Showing all {TOTAL} entries.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="border-ink border-t-[2.5px] pt-4">
            <p
              class="mono mb-2 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70"
            >
              Jump to
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              {
                JUMP.map((j) => (
                  <a
                    href={j.href}
                    class="border-ink bg-bg-alt mono shadow-neo-xs hover:bg-yellow hover:shadow-neo-sm rounded-full border-[2px] px-3 py-1 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase transition-all hover:-translate-x-0.5 hover:-translate-y-0.5"
                  >
                    {j.label}
                  </a>
                ))
              }
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="border-ink border-t-[2.5px] pt-4">
            <p
              class="mono mb-2 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70"
            >
              Badges
            </p>
            <ul class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
              {
                (["req", "res", "both"] as const).map((d) => (
                  <li
                    class:list={[
                      "mono border-ink border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                      DIR_CLASS[d],
                    ]}
                  >
                    {DIR_LABEL[d]}
                  </li>
                ))
              }
              {
                (["legacy", "de-facto", "draft", "dead"] as const).map(
                  (flag) => (
                    <li
                      class:list={[
                        "mono border-ink border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                        FLAG_CLASS[flag],
                      ]}
                    >
                      {FLAG_LABEL[flag]}
                    </li>
                  ),
                )
              }
              <li class="text-[12px] leading-snug opacity-70">
                De facto means no RFC defines it and everyone ships it anyway.
                Dead means delete it.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </NeoCard>
      </div>

      <div id="http-headers-empty" class="mt-6" hidden>
        <NeoCard tone="yellow" shadow="md">
          <p class="heading text-xl uppercase">
            Nothing matches “<span id="http-headers-term"></span>”
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            The filter matches the header name, the description, the example
            value and a few synonyms. Try a field name (<span class="mono"
              >etag</span
            >), a concept (<span class="mono">preflight</span>, <span
              class="mono">client ip</span
            >), or a status code (<span class="mono">429</span>).
          </p>
        </NeoCard>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Caching -->
    <section
      id="caching"
      data-header-group
      class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14"
    >
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">Caching and revalidation</h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Caching headers are the highest-leverage lines of config you will
          write all quarter. A correct <span class="mono">Cache-Control</span> on
          a hot endpoint removes more load than a week of query tuning, and a wrong
          one serves the wrong body to the wrong person from a machine you do not
          operate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two mental models make the rest of this section obvious. First,
          freshness is measured at the origin, not at you: a response with
          <span class="mono">max-age=600</span> that has been sitting in a CDN for
          nine minutes has sixty seconds left, and <span class="mono">Age</span> is
          how you find that out. Second, every response is stored under a key, and
          that key is the URL <em>plus</em> whatever <span class="mono"
            >Vary</span
          > says. Everything that goes wrong with caches is one of those two facts
          being ignored.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        data-header-table
        class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
      >
        <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">Caching and revalidation headers</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-cyan">
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[14rem] border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Header</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >What it does</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[18rem] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Example</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              CACHING.map((h) => (
                <tr
                  data-header-row
                  data-header-keywords={h.keywords}
                  class="hover:bg-bg-alt align-top transition-colors"
                >
                  <th
                    scope="row"
                    class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left"
                  >
                    <span class="mono block text-[13px] leading-tight font-bold break-words">
                      {h.name}
                    </span>
                    <span
                      class:list={[
                        "mono border-ink mt-2 inline-block border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                        DIR_CLASS[h.dir],
                      ]}
                    >
                      {DIR_LABEL[h.dir]}
                    </span>
                    {h.flag && (
                      <span
                        class:list={[
                          "mono border-ink mt-1.5 block w-fit border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                          FLAG_CLASS[h.flag],
                        ]}
                      >
                        {FLAG_LABEL[h.flag]}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </th>
                  <td class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                    <p>{h.purpose}</p>
                    {h.note && (
                      <p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] leading-snug opacity-80">
                        {h.note}
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="border-ink border-b-[2px] p-3">
                    <code class="mono block text-[12px] leading-snug break-words">
                      {h.example}
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div data-header-prose class="mt-10 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <h3 class="heading text-xl uppercase">
          Cache-Control, directive by directive
        </h3>
        <p>
          Almost every caching argument I have sat through was really an
          argument about one of these words. They combine, they contradict each
          other, and several of them mean different things depending on which
          side of the exchange they appear on.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        data-header-table
        class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
      >
        <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">Cache-Control directives</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-yellow">
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[14rem] border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Directive</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >What it does</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[18rem] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Example</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              DIRECTIVES.map((h) => (
                <tr
                  data-header-row
                  data-header-keywords={h.keywords}
                  class="hover:bg-bg-alt align-top transition-colors"
                >
                  <th
                    scope="row"
                    class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left"
                  >
                    <span class="mono block text-[13px] leading-tight font-bold break-words">
                      {h.name}
                    </span>
                    <span
                      class:list={[
                        "mono border-ink mt-2 inline-block border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                        DIR_CLASS[h.dir],
                      ]}
                    >
                      {DIR_LABEL[h.dir]}
                    </span>
                  </th>
                  <td class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                    <p>{h.purpose}</p>
                    {h.note && (
                      <p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] leading-snug opacity-80">
                        {h.note}
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="border-ink border-b-[2px] p-3">
                    <code class="mono block text-[12px] leading-snug break-words">
                      {h.example}
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div data-header-prose class="mt-8 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <NeoCard tone="red" shadow="lg">
          <p class="mono text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
            ▸ Classic mistake #1
          </p>
          <h3 class="heading mt-2 text-xl uppercase">
            no-cache does not mean do not cache
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            <span class="mono">no-cache</span> means store it and revalidate before
            every reuse. The cache keeps a copy, sends a conditional request with
            the ETag, and usually gets back a 304 with no body at all. That is a fast,
            cheap, correct outcome and it is exactly what you want on HTML.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            The directive that means what people think <span class="mono"
              >no-cache</span
            > means is <span class="mono">no-store</span>. If a response
            contains a session token, an invoice, or someone's address, that is
            the one you need — and it should be paired with <span class="mono"
              >private</span
            >
            so an old intermediary that misreads one of them still gets the message.
          </p>
        </NeoCard>

        <NeoCard tone="red" shadow="lg">
          <p class="mono text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
            ▸ Classic mistake #2
          </p>
          <h3 class="heading mt-2 text-xl uppercase">
            A missing Vary poisons the edge
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            You gzip based on <span class="mono">Accept-Encoding</span>. You
            pick a language from <span class="mono">Accept-Language</span>. You
            echo the caller's <span class="mono">Origin</span> into <span
              class="mono">Access-Control-Allow-Origin</span
            >. Each of those makes the response depend on a request header — and
            if that header is not in <span class="mono">Vary</span>, the cache
            stores one variant under a key that does not mention it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Then a different client asks, gets a hit, and receives a Brotli body
            it cannot decode, a French page it cannot read, or another tenant's
            CORS grant. It looks random, it is not reproducible locally, and it
            is one header. Audit this whenever a response body depends on
            anything except the URL.
          </p>
        </NeoCard>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Conditional requests and ranges -->
    <section
      id="conditional"
      data-header-group
      class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14"
    >
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">
        Conditional requests and ranges
      </h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The validators in the previous section save bandwidth. The ones here
          save correctness. <span class="mono">If-Match</span> on a PUT or PATCH turns
          a blind overwrite into a compare-and-swap: the server accepts the write
          only while the ETag you read still matches, and returns 412 otherwise. That
          is the difference between two tabs merging and two tabs silently destroying
          each other's edits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ranges are the other half. <span class="mono">Range</span> plus
          <span class="mono">If-Range</span> is how video seeking and resumable downloads
          work: ask for the bytes you are missing, and let the server hand back the
          whole file instead if it changed underneath you. Your static file server
          and CDN already implement all of this; the reason to know it is so you can
          tell whether a 206 in your logs is a feature or an attack.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        data-header-table
        class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
      >
        <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Conditional request and range headers
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-green">
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[14rem] border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Header</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >What it does</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[18rem] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Example</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              CONDITIONAL.map((h) => (
                <tr
                  data-header-row
                  data-header-keywords={h.keywords}
                  class="hover:bg-bg-alt align-top transition-colors"
                >
                  <th
                    scope="row"
                    class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left"
                  >
                    <span class="mono block text-[13px] leading-tight font-bold break-words">
                      {h.name}
                    </span>
                    <span
                      class:list={[
                        "mono border-ink mt-2 inline-block border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                        DIR_CLASS[h.dir],
                      ]}
                    >
                      {DIR_LABEL[h.dir]}
                    </span>
                  </th>
                  <td class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                    <p>{h.purpose}</p>
                    {h.note && (
                      <p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] leading-snug opacity-80">
                        {h.note}
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="border-ink border-b-[2px] p-3">
                    <code class="mono block text-[12px] leading-snug break-words">
                      {h.example}
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Content -->
    <section
      id="content"
      data-header-group
      class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14"
    >
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">
        Content negotiation and payload
      </h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          This group describes the body: what format it is in, how it was
          compressed, how long it is, and what the client would have preferred.
          Most APIs use about a third of it and ignore the rest, which is a
          defensible choice — the trouble starts when you half-implement
          negotiation and forget the <span class="mono">Vary</span> that makes it
          safe behind a cache.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two distinctions are worth burning in.
          <span class="mono">Content-Encoding</span> is end-to-end compression that
          the client decodes; <span class="mono">Transfer-Encoding</span> is per-hop
          framing that any proxy may undo. And <span class="mono"
            >Content-Location</span
          > is not <span class="mono">Location</span>: one tells you where the
          representation you just got actually lives, the other tells the client
          to go somewhere else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        data-header-table
        class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
      >
        <table class="w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Content negotiation and payload headers
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-cyan">
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[14rem] border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Header</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >What it does</th
              >
              <th
                scope="col"
                class="mono border-ink w-[18rem] border-b-[3px] p-3 text-[10px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase"
                >Example</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              CONTENT.map((h) => (
                <tr
                  data-header-row
                  data-header-keywords={h.keywords}
                  class="hover:bg-bg-alt align-top transition-colors"
                >
                  <th
                    scope="row"
                    class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-left"
                  >
                    <span class="mono block text-[13px] leading-tight font-bold break-words">
                      {h.name}
                    </span>
                    <span
                      class:list={[
                        "mono border-ink mt-2 inline-block border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                        DIR_CLASS[h.dir],
                      ]}
                    >
                      {DIR_LABEL[h.dir]}
                    </span>
                    {h.flag && (
                      <span
                        class:list={[
                          "mono border-ink mt-1.5 block w-fit border-[2px] px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-widest uppercase",
                          FLAG_CLASS[h.flag],
                        ]}
                      >
                        {FLAG_LABEL[h.flag]}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </th>
                  <td class="border-ink border-r-[3px] border-b-[2px] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                    <p>{h.purpose}</p>
                    {h.note && (
                      <p class="mt-1.5 text-[13px] leading-snug opacity-80">
                        {h.note}
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td class="border-ink border-b-[2px] p-3">
                    <code class="mono block text-[12px] leading-snug break-words">
                      {h.example}
                    </code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CORS -->
    <section id="cors" data-header-group class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14">
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">CORS and the preflight</h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          CORS is a browser mechanism for relaxing the same-origin policy, and
          nearly all of the confusion around it comes from reading it backwards.
          The default is that a page may <em>send</em> a cross-origin request but
          may not <em>read</em> the response. CORS headers are the origin server saying
          "this particular caller is allowed to read me". They do not gate the request;
          they gate the reply.
        </p>
        <p>
          Anything beyond a simple GET or form-shaped POST triggers a preflight:
          a separate <span class="mono">OPTIONS</span> request carrying
          <span class="mono">Origin</span> and
          <span class="mono">Access-Control-Request-Method</span>, plus
          <span class="mono">Access-Control-Request-Headers</span> if you set anything
          non-safelisted. The browser will not send the real request until that comes
          back approved, which is why one extra custom header can double your request
          count and add a round-trip to every call. Raise
          <span class="mono">Access-Control-Max-Age</span> and most of that cost goes
          away.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        data-header-table
        class="border-ink bg-paper shadow-neo-md mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[4px] border-[3px]"
