      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">Rate limiting and retries</h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A 429 with no headers is a wall in the dark. The client learns it was
          refused and nothing about when to come back, so it retries
          immediately, gets refused again, and turns your rate limiter into a
          load generator. One <span class="mono">Retry-After</span> fixes that, and
          it is the single highest-value header in this section.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond that there are two competing conventions. The
          <span class="mono">X-RateLimit-</span> triplet is the de-facto original,
          specified nowhere, with no agreement on whether the reset value is a Unix
          timestamp or a countdown. The IETF work — <span class="mono"
            >RateLimit</span
          > and <span class="mono">RateLimit-Policy</span> — fixes that with structured
          fields and explicit parameters, but it is still a draft and the shape has
          changed between revisions. If you run a public API, emit both, document
          the unit in plain words, and remember to list them in
          <span class="mono">Access-Control-Expose-Headers</span> or your browser
          clients will never see them.
        </p>
      </div>

