    <section id="proxy" data-header-group class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14">
      <h2 class="display text-2xl uppercase">
        Proxies, forwarding, connection
      </h2>
      <div data-header-prose class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The moment anything sits in front of your application — a load
          balancer, a CDN, an ingress controller — the connection your code sees
          is not the connection the user made. The client IP belongs to the
          proxy, the scheme is plain HTTP because TLS was terminated a hop ago,
          and the Host may have been rewritten. The
          <span class="mono">X-Forwarded-</span> family exists to carry the original
          facts across that gap, and <span class="mono">Forwarded</span> is the standardised
          version nobody quite finished adopting.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every one of these is written by whoever is upstream, which includes
          the caller. That is the whole problem, and it is covered below.
        </p>
      </div>

